>
  <h2 id="present-state">What Is the Present State of Web Accessibility in 2026?</h2>
  <p>
    The most thorough yearly measurement of the web is <a href="https://webaim.org/projects/million/">the WebAIM Million</a>, which runs an automated accessibility check on the home pages of the top one million websites. Its 2026 results show that the present state of web accessibility has slipped:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>95.9%</strong> of home pages had detectable failures of the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2, up from 94.8% in 2025.</li>
    <li>Pages averaged <strong>56.1 detected errors</strong>, up from 51 the year before, an increase of 10.1%.</li>
    <li>More than <strong>56 million</strong> distinct errors were detected across the million pages.</li>
    <li>Only about <strong>4 in 100</strong> home pages (4.1%) had no detected errors, which is simply 100% minus 95.9%.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    For anyone responsible for a website, the message is that a typical page today has dozens of accessibility problems that a machine can find, and that the trend is going the wrong way.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how-we-built-this">How We Built This Research Report</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>This report does not present new testing by AccessBell.</strong> We gathered the most reliable published 2026 data, checked each figure against its source, and added analysis and fix guidance from our own accessibility work. Where a number appears, the source is named. Some limits to keep in mind:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Home pages only.</strong> The WebAIM Million tests the home page of each site, not whole sites. Inner pages such as checkout and account areas may be better or worse.</li>
    <li><strong>Automated detection only.</strong> Automated tools find many problems but not all. A page with no detected errors is not necessarily accessible. Our <a href="/methodology">methodology page</a> explains what automation can and cannot cover.</li>
    <li><strong>A snapshot of popular sites.</strong> The top million sites are not every site. Smaller and newer sites may look different.</li>
    <li><strong>Year-on-year comparisons</strong> use the figures WebAIM published for 2025 and 2026.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>We looked for a second large 2026 dataset to cross-check WebAIM and did not find one we could verify, so we have not padded this report with weaker numbers.</p>

  <h2 id="numbers">Web Accessibility in 2026: The Numbers</h2>
  <h3>Almost every home page has failures</h3>
