  <h2 id="six-failures">The Six Failures Behind the State of Web Accessibility</h2>
  <p>
    The same six kinds of error have led the WebAIM results for seven years in a row, and they account for 96% of everything detected. That concentration is good news: fix these six and you clear most of the problems on the page.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <img src="/images/research/common-failures-2026.svg" width="800" height="494" loading="lazy" alt="Bar chart of the share of home pages with each failure: low contrast text 83.9 percent, missing image alt text 53.1, missing form input labels 51.0, empty links 45.4, empty buttons 29.6 and missing document language 15.8.">
    <figcaption>The six most common failures on home pages, 2026. Source: WebAIM Million.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Failure</th><th scope="col">Home pages</th><th scope="col">WCAG criterion</th><th scope="col">The fix</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Low contrast text</th><td>83.9%</td><td><a href="/blog/wcag-1-4-3-contrast-minimum">1.4.3 Contrast (Minimum)</a></td><td>Reach 4.5:1 for body text. Check with our <a href="/resources/contrast-checker">contrast checker</a>.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Missing image alt text</th><td>53.1%</td><td><a href="/blog/wcag-1-1-1-non-text-content">1.1.1 Non-text Content</a></td><td>Describe informative images, and use empty alt for decorative ones.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Missing form input labels</th><td>51.0%</td><td><a href="/blog/wcag-3-3-2-labels-or-instructions">3.3.2 Labels or Instructions</a></td><td>Give every field a visible, connected label.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Empty links</th><td>45.4%</td><td><a href="/blog/wcag-4-1-2-name-role-value">4.1.2 Name, Role, Value</a></td><td>Give icon and image links a text name.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Empty buttons</th><td>29.6%</td><td><a href="/blog/wcag-4-1-2-name-role-value">4.1.2 Name, Role, Value</a></td><td>Give icon-only buttons an accessible name.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Missing document language</th><td>15.8%</td><td><a href="/resources/wcag/3-1-1-language-of-page">3.1.1 Language of Page</a></td><td>Add <code>lang</code> to the <code>html</code> element.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Low contrast text alone was found on 83.9% of pages, up from 79.1% in 2025, with an average of 34 instances per home page.</p>

  <h2 id="what-it-means">What the 2026 Numbers Tell Us</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>The basics are still not done.</strong> Alt text, labels and contrast are among the oldest requirements in WCAG, yet each is missing on a large share of pages.</li>
    <li><strong>The problem is concentrated.</strong> Because six types cause 96% of errors, a focused clean-up beats a scattergun approach.</li>
    <li><strong>Most of it can be found automatically.</strong> These six are exactly what a scanner reports well. There is little excuse for shipping them, and continuous scanning stops them coming back.</li>
    <li><strong>Automation is the floor, not the ceiling.</strong> Real usability, including keyboard access, meaningful alt text and clear content, still needs people to test. Our <a href="/blog/keyboard-accessibility-testing">keyboard testing guide</a> is a quick place to start.</li>
    <li><strong>Standing still means falling behind.</strong> Errors per page rose 10.1% in a year, so sites that do not monitor tend to accumulate problems as content and code change.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="context">Why Web Accessibility Matters in 2026</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>People.</strong> The World Health Organization estimates that <a href="https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health">1.3 billion people, or 16% of the global population</a>, experience a significant disability. Our <a href="/blog/stories-of-web-users-with-disabilities">stories of web users with disabilities</a> show what the failures above feel like in practice.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Law.</strong> In the United States, the Department of Justice's rule for state and local government websites adopted WCAG 2.1 Level AA. In April 2026 the DOJ moved its compliance dates back a year, to <strong>April 26, 2027</strong> for entities serving 50,000 people or more and <strong>April 26, 2028</strong> for smaller ones, as summarised by <a href="https://www.reedsmith.com/articles/doj-extends-digital-accessibility-compliance-dates-under-title-ii-of-the-ada/">Reed Smith</a>. The requirements themselves did not change. Private businesses fall under Title III of the ADA, where the DOJ has said the ADA applies to websites but has not set a technical standard: see our guides to <a href="/blog/ada-title-iii-law-for-businesses">ADA Title III law for businesses</a> and the <a href="/blog/ada-website-compliance-guide">ADA website compliance guide</a>. In the European Union, the European Accessibility Act has applied since 28 June 2025, and our <a href="/resources/en-301-549-checker">EN 301 549 checker</a> tests against the related standard.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Business.</strong> Inaccessible pages lose customers, add legal risk and are harder to maintain. We look at one of the upsides in <a href="/resources/web-accessibility-and-seo-impact">what impact web accessibility has on SEO</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-to-do">How to Improve Web Accessibility on Your Site</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Measure.</strong> Run a <a href="/#scan">free WCAG scan</a> of your home page and two key journeys, such as checkout and contact.</li>
    <li><strong>Fix the six.</strong> Start with contrast, alt text, labels, link and button names, and the language attribute. Most fixes are small template changes.</li>
    <li><strong>Test by hand.</strong> Use only a keyboard, try a screen reader and check that nothing depends on color alone. Follow our <a href="/blog/wcag-2-aa-checklist">WCAG 2 AA checklist</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Publish a statement.</strong> Use our free <a href="/resources/statement-generator">accessibility statement generator</a> to say what you support and how people can reach you.</li>
    <li><strong>Monitor.</strong> New pages and plugins bring new errors. <a href="/app/signup">Start a 3-day free trial</a> and AccessBell Pro rescans up to 500 URLs per domain every day and alerts you when something regresses.</li>
  </ol>
</ResearchReport>
